 17592186045417] [17592186221462 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221462 #edb/ref 1050 1200.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221462 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d3cfa4d0-9bee-52e4-bb9e-39444a417cfd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221931 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186390825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221931 #edb/ref 1069 "q-328705" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221931 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221931 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{2t}f(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{2t}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221931 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c13572d-a427-531e-bc5a-c3129ec5167c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221931 #edb/ref 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{2t}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{2t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-2)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-2)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=F(s-2),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-2>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222357 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222357 #edb/ref 1061 600094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222357 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23a4eff8-b2bf-51f0-87ea-b1466d7af3ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222357 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222358 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222358 #edb/ref 1061 600095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222358 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3390cec0-8a87-5c98-86f2-ba843c736c0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222358 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222359 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222359 #edb/ref 1061 600096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222359 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fc54e4ac-72ee-57e2-8339-74eb6e586087" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222359 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222360 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222360 #edb/ref 1061 600100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222360 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "58c13b52-ac11-56f0-9289-23be6f240ad2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222360 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222361 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222361 #edb/ref 1061 600103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222361 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c7227d83-b5a7-5418-8e1d-915b257ae283" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222361 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222362 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222362 #edb/ref 1061 600104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222362 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b89a14ea-72ce-5ea2-90f1-e3c54fe52d78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222362 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222363 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222363 #edb/ref 1061 600108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222363 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3f432000-4480-52b3-b24b-72186edeb9f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222363 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222364 #edb/ref 1061 600111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222364 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aa6dc187-37cc-58ad-bd19-cba1f304979e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222364 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222476 #edb/ref 1064 "In a previous lesson, we learned how to compute Laplace transforms. We now examine how the Laplace transform changes when a function is multiplied by an exponential.\n\nSuppose\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nexists for $s>s_0.$ We want to compute the Laplace transform of $e^{at}f(t).$\n\nStarting from the definition of the Laplace transform,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,{e}^{at}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-a)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}\\,f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t,\n\\qquad s>s_0.\n$$\n\nComparing the two integrals, we see that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-(s-a)t}\\,f(t)\\,\\textrm{d}t\n=F(s-a).\n$$\n\nFor this to make sense, we need $s-a>s_0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\ns>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nThis result is known as the **First Shifting Theorem**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}f(t)\\} = F(s-a).\n$$\n\nIn the next slide, we will apply this result to a concrete example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222476 #edb/ref 1065 14326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222476 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "44078a68-8d27-5f6e-adec-348668ecef51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222476 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222508 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the first shifting theorem states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = F(s),\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{e^{at}f(t)\\right\\}  = F(s-a).\n$$\n\nThis means the new Laplace transform is the original graph of $F$ evaluated at $s-a$.\n\nSo, the graph shift is:\n\n- If $a>0$, then $F(s-a)$ is the graph of $F(s)$ **shifted to the right** by $a$ units.\n\n- If $a<0$, then $F(s-a)$ is the graph of $F(s)$ **shifted to the left** by $|a|$ units.\n\nFor example, suppose $F(s)$ has the following graph:\n\n![](images/45/45ce62c981e4c7853ef158046eb21fbc9c135f5fa0a8887d813a07594077c8ce.png)\n\nThen the Laplace transform of $e^{-3t} f(t)$ is $F(s+3).$ Here, $a=-3$, so this corresponds to shifting the graph of $F(s)$ three units to the left:\n\n![](images/b7/b75658824079fe90c9c4f43d4d37d355c95b845ebed3f11bdf751b52f8d34f55.png)\n\nA useful self-check is that any horizontal features move the same way:\n\n- vertical asymptotes shift left/right by the same amount,\n\n- intercepts and key points shift left/right by the same amount." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222508 #edb/ref 1065 14589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222508 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "db14b380-2e8e-5007-a9ad-722a83b574ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222508 #edb/ref 1067 "Graph Shifting" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222509 #edb/ref 1064 "The **First shifting Theorem** states that if\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nfor $s>s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a),\n\\qquad s>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nSo, when you multiply a function by $e^{at}$, you do two things:\n\n- *Replace* $s$ with $s-a$ in the known transform $F(s)$.\n\n- *Shift the domain* from $s>s_0$ to $s>s_0+a$.\n\nTo see this in action, consider the following example.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2},\n\\qquad s>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose we want to compute\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{2t}\\sin(3t)\\}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we identify the base transform:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin(3t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s^2+3^2}\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the first shifting theorem with $a=2$, which means we replace $s$ by $s-2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\} & =\\frac{3}{(s-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(s-2)^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the original transform is valid for $s>0$, the shifted transform is valid for\n\n$$\ns>2.\n$$\n\nThis example illustrates the general pattern: multiplying by $e^{at}$ leads to a simple substitution $s:= s-a$, along with a corresponding shift in the domain." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222509 #edb/ref 1065 14590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222509 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "511f0e8e-f19d-5e1e-955e-97dbd8386ea2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222509 #edb/ref 1067 "Using the First Shifting Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222520 #edb/ref 1064 "Using the first shifting theorem, we can obtain new Laplace transforms from old ones without performing any additional integration.\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)\n$$\n\nfor $s>s_0$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a),\n\\qquad s>s_0+a.\n$$\n\nThe table below shows some general results we can obtain using the first shifting theorem.\n\n| $f(t)$ | $F(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\}$ | Compare With |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $e^{at} t^n$ | $\\dfrac{n!}{(s-a)^{n+1}}$ | $\\mathcal L \\{t^n\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}$ |\n| $e^{at}\\sin \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{\\omega}{(s-a)^2+\\omega^2}$ | $\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ |\n| $e^{at}\\cos \\omega t$ | $\\dfrac{s-a}{(s-a)^2+\\omega^2}$ | $\\mathcal L\\{\\cos\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ |\n\nSo, each new entry in our table is obtained by taking a known transform and replacing $s$ with $s-a$, and then shifting the domain accordingly." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222520 #edb/ref 1065 14692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222520 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dbf3c06b-f0f4-528b-aff6-5b6adcae65b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222520 #edb/ref 1067 "A Laplace Transforms Comparison Table" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302546 #edb/ref 1080 "s>{s}_{0}-9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302546 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302546 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d813493-43b7-5abc-9c6e-eebce057cbdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302554 #edb/ref 1080 "D(s-9)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302554 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302554 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0eea1ccd-542e-57f0-9eb9-ed18e4ca2120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302582 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{(s-9)t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302582 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302582 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "12f90244-ded0-5104-9e2a-acaa009da0ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302590 #edb/ref 1080 "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $8$ horizontally." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302590 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302590 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14a729c4-4d54-527d-a93f-3a54c3407e68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302602 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-9t}d(t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302602 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302602 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "16979a0a-5fab-5b5c-86c2-4f69494ac5bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302621 #edb/ref 1080 "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $8$ vertically." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302621 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302621 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "18dea247-7ffa-5cb9-8234-c442fc60d964" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302668 #edb/ref 1080 "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $8$ units to the right." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302668 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302668 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "21cdcb93-5dad-510a-96e8-42da2d959713" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302726 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{st}\\cdot {e}^{9t}d(t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302726 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302726 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2a895488-234e-5a29-b81f-90f3846bbf81" #edb/r